#
# Managed by Ansible -- ansible/roles/basetool_host (ADR-0188). Edits are overwritten.
#
# Rationale: docs/PODMAN_HOST_BOOTSTRAP.md section 6.
#
# IT CARRIED NO SETTINGS UNTIL 2026-09-22, ON PURPOSE, and the paragraph explaining the emptiness
# is kept below because the question it answers -- "shouldn't this set rootless_port_forwarder?" --
# is still asked, and the answer is still no. What changed is a different setting entirely.
#
# rootless_port_forwarder is DELIBERATELY NOT SET here, and the omission is the decision.
#
# Setting it to "pasta" was the entire reason this migration first moved to a platform with podman 6.
# Measured on 2026-09-16, that forwarder preserves the client address on IPv4 and delivers NO IPv6 at
# all: the socket binds, the connection is reset, nothing reaches the container. Traced to podman
# overriding the rootless namespace's IPv6 address with a ULA, so the host's global v6 address is not
# local there and netavark's `fib daddr type local` DNAT chain is never entered.
#
# ADR-0187 recovers the client address a different way -- a host-level haproxy hands it to the edge
# in a PROXY protocol header -- which needs no forwarder setting at all. The default, rootlessport,
# is what runs, and the edge no longer cares what address it presents.
#
# If a future podman fixes the IPv6 path, turning this on is still not a free win: the edge would
# then trust two sources for one fact. The acceptance is the BEHAVIOUR either way, via
# scripts/check-conformance.py's client-address-visible check.

# --- the second address a container can reach this host at (ADR-0196, extended 2026-09-22) -------
#
# A rootless container cannot reach its own host at the host's public address -- the bridge lives in
# the service user's netns and outbound leaves through pasta, which does not fold the connection
# back. ADR-0196 answers that by aliasing every public name to `host-gateway`, which podman
# implements by starting the rootless netns pasta with `--map-guest-addr 169.254.1.2`.
#
# ONE address, and IPv4. That is why the IPv6 blackbox probes could not run: `http_2xx_ipv6` pins
# `preferred_ip_protocol: ip6` with no fallback, the alias offers only an A record, and the probe
# fails locally with "no suitable address found" -- against an edge serving IPv6 perfectly well
# (measured from the host itself: 302, 302, 401 in tens of milliseconds). `EdgeIpv6Unreachable`
# then fires about the monitoring rather than about the edge, which is the worst kind of alert.
#
# MEASURED on the testing host 2026-09-22, same Rocky 10.2 and same podman 5.8.2 as production:
#
#   * `pasta_options` DOES reach the rootless netns pasta, not only `--network=pasta` containers;
#   * a v6 `--map-guest-addr` works -- the mapped address went from unreachable to open;
#   * podman REPLACES its own `--map-guest-addr 169.254.1.2` rather than appending to it. In the
#     run that set only the v6 mapping, `host-gateway` went from open to UNREACHABLE. So the v4
#     entry below is not redundant: leaving it out breaks every ADR-0196 alias at once.
#   * with both, both families work, and a real named HTTPS request over the mapped v6 answered
#     200 (and 302 on /healthz) -- byte-identical to its v4 twin in the same container.
#
# Only a container with an IPv6 route can use the v6 entry; today that is blackbox-exporter, on
# net-blackbox-v6. For everything else the entry is inert, which is why it is safe to set globally.
[network]
pasta_options = [{% for a in basetool_host_pasta_map_guest_addrs %}"--map-guest-addr", "{{ a }}"{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}]
